## 1. Introduction
The pyruvate dehydrogenase (PDHaset) multienzyme complex catalyses the oxidative decarboxylation of pyruvate, producing carbon dioxide and acetyl-CoA. The complex obtained from Bacillus stearothermophilus is built around an icosahedral structural core consisting of 60 copies of the dihydrolipoamide acetyltransferase polypeptide chain $\{{\mathrm{E}}{\mathrm{}}{\mathrm{}}\}$ ; EC 2.3.l.12; Henderson et al., l979). To this central core are bound multiple copies of a pyruvate decarboxylase (Elp, pyruvate dehydrogenase (lipoamide), EC l.2.4.l) and dihydrolipoamide dehydrogenase (E3; EC 1.8.1.4; Perham & Packman, 1989; Perham, 1991).
The Elp component of PDHase complexes catalyses the decarboxylation of pyruvate and the reductive transfer of the acetyl moiety to the lipoyl lysine residue of the E2p subunit (Reed, 1974). It can exist in one of two forms, either as a dimer of a single polypeptide chain, as in Escherichia coli, or as a heterodimer made up of two α and two $\beta$ polypeptide chains, as in $B$ . stearothermophilus (Perham & Packman, l989; Patel & Roche, I990; Perham, 1991). The $\alpha$ -chain is believed to be responsible for binding thiamin diphosphate (ThDP; Stepp & Reed, 1985), which is the coenzyme involved in the decarboxylation step (Walsh, 1979; Kluger, 1987). In support of this theory a common structural motif present in ThDP-binding enzymes, which structure predictions indicate to be a $\beta\alpha\beta$ -fold, has been found in all Elα subunits studied to date (Hawkins et al.. 1989). A recently determined crystal structure of transketolase (Lindqvist et al., 1992), another ThDP-utilizing enzyme, confirms the involvement of this motif in binding the cofactor.
As indicated above, the structural and mechanistic heart of the PDHase complex is provided by $\bf E2p$ , which has an unusual multidomain structure (Reed & Hackert, 1990; Perham, 199l). Treatment of the B. stearothermophilus PDHase complex with chymotrypsin releases the single lipoyl domain from the N terminus of each E2p chain without causing disassembly of the intact complex. This domain consists of approximately 80 residues and contains the lipoylated lysine residue (Duckworth et al., 1982).
The lipoyl domain is separated by a mobile linker sequence consisting of approximately 30 residues from another smaller domain of approximately 40 to 50 residues. Excision of this latter domain with, say,trypsin causes release of the Elp and E3 suburits (Duckworth et al., l982; Packman et al., 1988). A further linker sequence separates this peripheral subunit-binding domain from a large $\left\{\bf{28}~\mathbf{kDa}\right\}$ → C-terminal domain, which is responsible for aggregating to form the icosahedral core and which also houses the acetyltransferase active site. All E2 chains from other 2-oxo acid dehydrogenase complexes are similar in structure, except that the number of lipoyl domains can increase to two (e.g. eukaryotic PDHase complexes) or three (E. coli and other Gram-negative bacterial PDHase complexes: Perham & Packman, l989; Reed & Hackert, 1990; Perham, l99l). Another distinction worth noting is that in those complexes with an E2 core of octahedral (24-mer) symmetry, such as the PDHase and 2-oxoglutaratedehydrogenase(2OGDHase) complexes of $E$ . coli, excision of the peripheral subunit-binding domain causes loss only of the E3 subunits (Perham & Packman, 1989; Reed & Hackert, l990; Perham, 199l). This suggests that a major part at least of the El binding site in these complexes must reside in the E2 catalytic domain.
So far it has proved impossible to crystallize any intact E2 component. However, the domain-andlinker structure lends itself well to an alternative approach of cumulative structural analysis. Thus the three-dimensional structures of the lipoyl domains from both B. stearothermophilus (Dardel et al., 199l, 1993) and E. coli (J. D. F. Green, E. D. Laue & R. N. Perham, unpublished results) PDHase complexes have been determined in this laboratory by means of muitidimensional n.m.r. spectroscopy. The structure of the E3-binding domain from the dihydrolipoamide succinyltransferase chain (E2o: EC 2.3.l.6l) of the 2OGDHase complex from $E$ . coli has also been determined by means of n.m.r. spectroscopy with a root-mean-square deviation from the mean co-ordinates of l24 A (Robien et $a l$ 1992). The structure of the C-terminal catalytic domain of $\mathbf{E2p}$ from A. vinelandii has been solved by means of X-ray crystallography to a resolution of 2·6 $\mathbf{\AA}$ (Mattevi et al., 1992b).
In this paper we present the determination of the three-dimensional structure of the peripheral subunit-binding domain of the E2 chain of $B$ . stearothermophilus PDHase complex. As explained above, this differs from the peripheral subunit-binding domain of $E$ . coli E2o in that it has a major part to play in binding the El component.
By combining the results of this paper with previous n.m.r. and crystallographic studies of the constituent domains of the $\mathrm{\bar{E}2p/\mathrm{E20}}$ chains from $E$ . coli and the $\mathrm{E2p}$ chain from $B$ . stearothermophilus, it may become possible to piece together a picture of the overall structure of E2. This should provide new insight into the interplay of protein domains essential in this complex enzyme assembly
## 2. Materials and Methods
## (a) Peptide synthesis
A peptide of 43 amino acid residues starting at Val128 and finishing at Alal70 (Borges et al., l990) was synthesized on an Applied Biosystems 430A peptide synthesizer (Merrifieldetal.,982).Thiscontainedthe E3/Elp-binding domain as defined by disassembly of the intact complex following limited proteolysis (Packman et al.. l988). The peptide was cleaved from the solid support by using a low/high HF cleavage method (Tam et al. 1983). This 2-step process is necessary sinee it is not possible in all cases to remove protecting groups by a single low HF step. After extraction with $5\%(\mathrm{v/v})$ ethanoic acid, the peptide was purified by reverse-phase highpressure liquid chromatography (HPLC) on a Vydac C4 semi-preparative column using an acetonitrile/water solve nt gradient containing $0{\cdot}1\%$ trifluoroethanoic acid.
