## (c) Nuclear magnetic resonance assignments
Sequential resonance assignments were made using standard 2D n.m.r. procedures (Wuthrich, 1986; Englander & Wand, l987). The list of proton resonance assignments is given in Table 1.
## (d) Determination of distance restraints
NOEs were assigned using the ${\mathfrak{g o}}_{\mathbf{ms}}$ and $\mathbf{200ms}$ spectra. They were classified into the following categories: strong, medium, weak and very weak. These corresponded to the following inter-proton distance restraints: 18 to 2·7, 1-8 to 3·3, 18 to 5-0 and 18 to $\mathbf{6{\cdot}0\mathrm{~\AA~}}$ , respectively. A measure of the contribution of spin diffusion to the crosspeak intensities was obtained by application of a Taylor transform to the NOEsY build-up spectra $\{\mathfrak{t}_{\mathfrak{m}}=$ 25,50, 75 and $\mathbf{l00ms}$ ) and examining the signs of the first and second order coefficients (Hyberts $\dot{\alpha}$ Wagner, 1989). It was found that the effects of spin diffusion were small up to a mixing time of $\mathbf{l00ms}$ .Thus most of the distance restraints were derived from NOESY spectra recorded with a mixing time of $90\mathrm{ms}$ in preference to $\mathbf{200}\mathrm{{ms}}$ , ensuring that the contribution of spin diffusion to crosspeak intensities was, for the most part, negligible.
