## (f) Hydrogen bond restraints
Hydrogen exchange rates were determined using 4 TOCSY spectra recorded over a period of ${\bf l}\mathbf{2}\mathbf{h}$ . Hydrogen bond restraints corresponding to interatomie distances of $\mathbf{l}{\cdot}\mathbf{8}{<}\mathbf{N}\mathbf{H}{\ldots}{\mathbf{0}}{<}\mathbf{2}{\cdot}\mathbf{5}\mathrm{~\AA~}$ and $\mathbf{2{\cdot}8{<}N{\dots}O{<}3{\cdot}5}\ .$ A were imposed for slowly exchanging backbone amide protons where the acceptors could be identified. This proved to be a nontrivial process (see Results and Diseussion).
## 'g) Stereospecific assignments and $\chi^{1}$ angle restraints
Stereospecific assignments of the diastereotopic $\beta$ methylene protons of amino acid residue side-chains were made by analysis of the vicinal $3J_{\alpha\beta}$ coupling constants and the relative intensities of intra-residue NOEs involving the HN, $\mathbf{H}^{\alpha}$ and $\mathbf{H}^{\pmb{\beta}}$ 111 $\mathbf{H}^{\gamma}$ in the case of valine and isoleucine residues) protons (Arseniev et al., l988; Wagner et al., 1987; Hyberts et al., 1987; Zuiderweg et $a l$ , 1985). $^3J_{\alpha\beta}$ coupling constants for all AMX residues,2 outof 3 isoleucine residues, 4 out of 5 valine residues and the lone threonine residue were measured directly from the E-COSY spectrum. It was assumed that the amino acid side-chains adopted 1 of the 3 staggered rotamers with $\chi^{1}=+60^{\circ}$ p $180^{\circ}$ or $-60^{\circ}$ . Only those $5J_{\alpha\beta}$ vicinal coupling constants where both $^3J_{\alpha\beta}$ values were consistent with l of the 3 staggered rotamers were used to generate dihedral angle restraints (Nagayama & Withrich, 1981).
## (h) Structure calculations
Structures were caiculated from random starting structures by using a biphasic dynamical simulated annealing protocol. The first phase was based primarily on that of Nilges et al. (l988). The second performed a search of the potential energy surface in the vicinity of the local minimum located in phase 1. Phase 2 involved reheating to l00oK whilst maintaining the near square well potential and performing I5 ps of dynamics. The system was then cooled to $\bf{300\bar{K}}$ in 56 ps of simulation. The protocol was terminated with a final energy minimization. The structures thus produced had good covalent geometry with negligible deviations from ideal stereochemistry. In this way, approximately $80\%$ of the runs were found to converge such that no violation ${\bf>0.5\mathrm{~\AA~}}$ was obtained in the resulting structures. In addition, the number of violations ${\bf>0.2\AA}$ was reduced by approximately $50\%$ · A biphasic protocol was preferred to a lengthened single protocol, since the structures obtained using the multicooling annealing schedule had fewer residual violations and a better ratio of converged to non-converged structures than those generated by a protocol with a single elongated cooling step.
The program XPLOR version 2.l (Bringer, 1990), whien is based on the moleeular dynamics program CHARMm (Brooks et al., l983), was used for all of the structure calculations. These were performed on a Meiko Computing Surface with 20 transputers. The structures were examined on an SGI 4D/70GT workstation using QUANTA release 3.2 (Polygen/Molecular Simulations) and on an Evans and Sutherland PS390 driven by a MICROVAX 3100 using the program HYDRA (Hubbard, 1986.
## 3. Results and Discussion
## (a) Comparison of the synthetic peptide with the native protein
Native E3/Elp-binding domain was generated by limited proteolysis with trypsin (Packman & Hipps, 1991) of a didomain obtained by over-expressing a sub-gene encoding the N-terminal 170 residues of thedihydrolipoamide acetyltransferase from B. stearothermophilus in E.coli (Hipps & Perham, 1992). In order to confirm the identity of the synthetic peptide with the native $\mathbf{\mathrm{E3/E1}}\mathbf{\mathrm{p}}$ -binding domain, two NOESY spectra with mixing times of 200 ms were recorded on a Bruker AMX600 spectrometer using $\mathbf{l}{\cdot}\mathbf{5}\ \mathbf{m}\mathbf{3}$ samples of the respective peptides. The spectra were essentially similar although the intensities of the crosspeaks in the spectrum from the native domain were weaker. This was εttributed to the co-purification of a proteinase in low concentration with the $\mathbf{E3}/\mathbf{E}\bar{\mathbf{l}}_{\mathrm{P}}$ -binding domain, which subsequently caused the partial degradation of the sample. The ${\mathrm{NH}}\langle\omega_{2}{\mathrm{-axis}}\rangle.$ $\mathrm{C}^{\alpha}(\tilde{\omega}_{1}{\cdot}\mathbf{axis})$ fingerprint regions were identical. The affinisy of the native and the synthetic peptides for E3 was investigated by using a number of methods including  polyacrylamide  gel  electrophoresis, capillary electrophoresis and gel filtration. These showed that both peptides bound E3 with comparable dissociation constants (D. S. H. & R. N. P., unpublished results).
## (b) Statistical analysis of the structures
A final set of 35 computed structures with low restraint violation energies was used as the basis for a detailed statistical analysis of the structure. Two criteria were used to select this final set of structures; first, none of these 35 structures had any violations ${\bf>0.35\mathrm{~\AA~}}$ ， and secondly, they were allowed only one violation ${\bf\gamma>0.3~\AA}$ . The superimposed backbone conformations of the final set of 35 structures are shown in Figure l.
All of the statistical calculations described below were carried out on the structured region of the binding domain extending from Val7 to Leu39. Outside this region there was a sharp increase in the average deviation of the main-chain torsion angles $\phi$ and $\Psi$ from their mean values. The average r.m.s.d. from the mean co-ordinates of the structured region of the backbone from $\mathbf{Val}7$ to Leu39 is 0.36 A. Upon including all side-chain heavy atoms, the r.m.s.d. from the mean co-ordinates is 0-96 A. If residues exhibiting conformational averaging as deduced from the values of $^3J_{\alpha\beta}$ coupling constants are excluded from the calculation, the r.m.s.d. from the mean co-ordinates for all the heavy atoms is 047 A (Table 2). The variation of the r.m.s.d. from the mean co-ordinates for both backbone and sidechain heavy atoms for each residue is illustrated in Figure 2. Although the region from Met4 to Ser6 shows no long-range NOE restraint and consequently lacks a well-defined structure, it does appear to adopt a group of preferred approximately right-handed helical conformations.
A Ramachandran plot of residues Val7 to Leu39 for all 35 structures shows most residues are restricted to allowed regions of $(\phi,\psi)$ space (Fig. 3). There is, of course, a preponderance of residues in regions characteristic of right-handed helical structure. The non-glycine residues that, in some of the structures, adopt positive $\phi$ are Lys26 and Asn27. The former sometimes adopts an $a_{1}$ conformation and the latter adopts an ε conformation. In no single calculated structure do both residues exhibit positive $\phi$ values. The present data therefore preclude high definition of the molecular structure at these two residue positions. The effect is entirely local, however, and no conformational uncertainty is propagated to the surrounding structure. There is no evidence to suggest that the lack of definition in the loop region $\mathrm{Gln22}$ to Leu3l is due to conformational flexibility rather than to a low density of interatomic distance restraints.
## (c) Architecture of the binding domain
## (i) Elements of secondary structure and related motifs
The secondary structural elements were identified by using the program NAOMI (Brocklehurst & Perham, l993). The delineation was made from a consideration of main-chain-main-chain hydrogen bonding patterns and three conformational parameters: the main-chain dihedral angles $\phi$ and $\psi$ and the angle defined by the atoms Ci-2,Ca: and Cai+2, where i is a residue number.
The well-defined region of the binding domain from Val7 to Leu39 forms a compact structure of approximate dimensions $25\mathrm{\AA}\times20\mathrm{\bar{A}}\times20\ .$ A (Fig. 4). The structure contains two short parallel $\alpha$ -helices. Helix I consists of residues from Val7 to Lysl4. It should be noted that although the classical repeating hydrogen bond pattern characteristic of an $\alpha$ -helix continues to Vali6, the presence of Glyl5, which adopts a positive value of $\phi$ in all 35 calculated structures, means the helix terminates at Lysl4. Helix II comprises residues Lys32 to Leu39. Althcugh a hydrogen bond is formed from Ala40 NH to Asp36 CO, the uncertainty in the main-chain dihedral angles of Ala40 relegates it to a position outside the well-defined region of the structure. Indeed, in 13 of the calculated structures it adopts a positive value of $\phi$ . The other main structural motifs are a distorted $\mathbf{3_{10}}$ -helix from Aspl7 to Val21 and a loop from $\mathbf{G}\mathbf{ln22}$ to Leu3l.The $\mathbf{3_{10}}$ -helix has classical $\mathbf{\delta}_{i,i+3}$ hydrogen bonds from Aspl7 CO to Leu20 NH and Ilel8 CO to Val21 NH. The structure of the loop is defined by Asp34, which forms hydrogen bonds with a series of four main-chain amide protons, those of Gly23, Gly25, Thr24 and Leu3l and the side-chain hydroxyl group of Thr24 (vide infra). In addition to these structural elements, two pairs of overlapping $\beta$ turns are observed. The first pair involves residues Gln22 to Gly25 and Gly23 to Lys26, and the second corresponds to Lys26-Arg29 and Asn27 to Val30 (Table 3). A hydrogen bond is observed from Val30 NH to Asn27 CO (Fig. 5) in 29 out of the 35 calculated structures.The conformational uncertainty described in the preceding section and evidenced by the variation of the values of $\phi$ and $\psi$ , is located in this second set of turns. The amino acid sequence and a survey of the sequential and medium range NOE connectivities are shown in Figure 6(a). The secondary structural elements are clearly defined by the characteristic NOE patterns in Figure $\delta(a)$ and (b), which also show long-range NOEs.
## (ii) Structure of the hydrophobic core
The side-chains of residues involved in forming attractive van der Waals contacts for all of the calculated structures were delineated by using NAOMI. Binary interactions of methine, methylene and methyl groups were considered since these groups are unlikely to be invoived in any other interactions. These groups were represented as sphereswithdiametersequal “to45 A. Interpenetration of these spheres was taken as an indication of a non-polar contact.
As expected, the side-chains of residues involved in forming the non-polar core are well defined (Fig. 7). The disorder observed in the side-chains of both Ilel8 and Ile35 arises from the uncertainty in the dihedral angle $x^{2}$ . A number of side-chains that are exposed to solvent appear to be ordered up to $\chi^{1}$ Helix I packs against helix II but not the $\mathbf{3_{10}-h e l i x},$ which makes non-polar contacts exclusively with helix II (Fig. 8). An important role is played by Vall6, which lies outside the defined secondary structural elements but makes contacts with residues from each of the three helices. Val2l from the $\displaystyle{\bf3}_{10}$ -helix forms interhelical contacts with helix II by packing against Ile35 and Phe38. The nonpolar contacts made by helix I are dominated by Ile35 and Phe38.
## (iii) Analysis of $^{1}H/^{2}H$ exchange spectra
The rates of exchange of the main-chain amide protons were determined by recording four TOCSY spectra over a period of 12 hours (Fig. 9), Owing to the compact, globular nature of the $\bf{E3/E l p}$ -binding domain and the high population of hydrogenbonded states, approximately $60\%$ of the mainchain amide protons were left unexchanged when the first spectrum was recorded (Fig. 9(a)). The main-chain amide protons from the N-terminal five residues and the C-terminal residue, Ala43, exhibited rapid exchange, as did those of Val7, Arg8 and Lys9 from the N-terminal region of helix I. The main-chain amide protons of Aspl7 and Ilel8, which are found in the irregular structure lying between Helix I and the $\mathbf{3_{10}}$ -helix, are not stabilized by hydrogen bond formation and, since they are accessible to solvent, exchange rapidly. Gln22, Lys26 and Asn27, which are found in the loop region, exhibit rapid exchange of their main-chain amide protons, as do Lys32 and Glu33 which are in the N-terminal portion of helix II and make no hydrogen bonds. When the second spectrum was recorded, after a further three hours, the main-chain
