amide protons from Ser6, Gly28 and Asp34, none of which formed hydrogen bonds, had been replaced by deuterons (Fig. 9(b)). In addition, the backbone amide protons of Gly23 and Gly25, which formed side-chain-main-chain hydrogen bonds with the carboxylate side-chain of Asp34, but had a high degree of solvent accessibility, had also been exchanged. When the third spectrum was recorded, the main-chain amide protons of Alall and Glyl5 had been replaced by deuterons (Fig. 9(c)). After 12 hours, when the final TOCSY spectrum was recorded, the amide protons of Tyrl0, Argl2 and Glul3 from helix I had fully exchanged whilst the crosspeak from the main-chain amide proton of Lysl4 was very weak (Fig. 9(d)). The main-chain amide proton of Arg29 from the loop region had exchanged whilst the signal from Thr24 NH was of considerably reduced intensity, indicating that a significant degree of deuteration had occurred. The backbone amide protons of Asp36 and Ala40 from the C-terminal region of helix II had also exchanged.
Although the main-chain amide protons of Ala40, Gly4l and Gly42 are clearly protected in the folded state, as evidenced by the slow rates of exchange, the deviation in the main-chain torsion angles and the lack of medium and long-range NOEs to the C-terminal residues suggest that a time-averaged representation of the region may be inappropriate; thus helix II is considered to terminate at Leu39. Furthermore, the crosspeaks exhibited by the mainchain amide protons of Gly4l and Gly42 are of weak intensity, indicating that although the hydrogenbonded states exist they are only sparsely populated. The remaining main-chain amide protons having crosspeaks of appreciable intensity are those from Vall6, Argl9, Leu20, Val21, Val30, Leu3l, Hle35, Ala37, Phe38 and Leu39. Although Vall6 and Val30 lie outside the secondary structural elements, they both form main-chain-main-chain hydrogen bonds,to Glyll and Asn27, respectively, and contribute significantly to the hydrophobic core and are completely solvent inaccessible. The main-chain amide proton of Argl9 makes a side-chain-mainchain hydrogen bond with the carboxylate sidechain of Aspl7. The amide protons of Leu20 and Val21, which are also in the $\bar{\mathbf{3}}_{\mathbf{10}}\mathbf{-}\mathbf{helix}$ , form characteristic $^{i,i-3}$ hydrogen bonds with Aspl7 CO and Hlel8 CO, respectively. The backbone amide proton of Leu3l forms a side-chain-main-chain hydrogen bond with the carboxylate side-chain of Asp34 and is inaccessible to solvent molecules. Of the remaining residues, Ala37, Phe38 and Leu39 form hydrogen bonds in helix II, and Ile35 is the most significant contributor to the non-polar core and is completely buried. Solvent accessibility calculations were performed by using the version of the Lee & Richards (l97l) aigorithm encoded in XPLOR and using a probe radius of 14 A.
## (iv) Identification of hydrogen-bond acceptors
The delineation of hydrogen-bond acceptors was not a simple task as the restraints available from the NOE data alone did not produce well-converged structures (see Robien et al., l992). Our final set of hydrogen-bond donor-acceptor pairs explains all of the hydrogen exchange and $^3J_{\alpha\beta}^{-}$ coupling constant data. In order to identify the acceptors for mainchain donors, we used the following iterative approach. Thirty structures with no violations $\bar{>}\bar{0}{\cdot}5$ A were calculated and each structure was examined to identify possible acceptors. Where the acceptor was unambiguous, we used this as a restraint for the next round of structure calculations. As more hydrogen-bond restraints were added, the conformations of the calculated structures were found to cluster around distinct average structures. In all cases these could be classified according to the conformations of certain residues. For example, structures could be subdivided into two classes depending on whether Gly23 adopted either positive or negative values of $\phi$ . For all such classes of structure, another 30 structures of each category were calculated before any conclusions regarding the identity of acceptors was made. The final restraint list leads to the only class of structures that could be found that satisfied all the experimental exchange data (Fig. l0). In addition, all the hydrogen bonds that are inferred from the final structures and were not put in as restraints have good geometry and appear from their geometry to have strengths consistent with those predicted from the exchange data (S. M. Brocklehurst, Y. N. Kalia & R. N. Perham, unpublished results). All the side-chain-main-chain hydro gen bonds on the surface of the protein correspond to residues that occupy a single rotamer state as evidenced by the $3J_{\alpha\beta}$ coupling constants. The delineation of hydrogen bonds and identification of the clusters of structures was facilitated by the program NAOMI.
It should be emphasized that although the NOE interproton distance restraints do not define all the hydrogen bonds in themselves, the careful iterative adjustment of the hydrogen-bond restraints meant that hydrogen-bond restraints were added only when the NOE data and the hydrogen-bond restraints from the previous round of structure calculations unambiguously defined acceptors for a main-chain proton donor. Although it is clear that the final set of structures depends critically on the hydrogen-bond restraints, it is important to note that more than $50\%$ of the hydrogen bonds formed in the structure were not restrained. Only the hydrogen-bond from Asn27 NH to Gly25 CO appears to form erroneously, as evidenced by the rapid exchange of Asn27 NH. It is clear that formation of this hydrogen-bond is not an absolute requirement for satisfaction of the final restraint list, given that it forms in 17 out of the 35 structures. This is an $^{i,i+2}$ hydrogen bond, however, which is likely to be considerably weaker than both $i,i+3$ and $i,i+4$ hydrogen bonds because of the unfavourable stereochemistry. It is conceivable, therefore, that this hydrogen bond could exist but be sufficiently weak to exchange rapidly under the experimental conditions.
Helix I contains $a$ classical hydrogen-bond network involving a series of $i,i+4$ bonds, with multiple acceptors being observed in the middle of the helix. At the N-terminal end, there is an $i,i+3$ hydrogen bond from $\mathbf{Val}7$ CO to TyrlO NH, whilst at the C-terminal end of the helix an $i,i+5$ hydrogen bond is formed between Alall CO and Vall6 NH. In addition to containing the characteristic $i,i+3$ hydrogen bonds from Aspl7 CO to Leu20 NH and Ilel8 CO to Val2l NH, the $\mathbf{3}_{10}$ -helix contains both side-chain-main-chain and side-chain-sidechain hydrogen bonds. Invariably these involve the carboxylate side-chain of Aspl7. Helix II deviates from the ideal geometry near Asp34, with Phe38 forming an $i,i-j$ bond with Ile35 in preference to the $i,i-4$ bond with Asp34. This distortion can be seen in the representation shown in Figure 4.
In all of the calculated structures, Asp34 is inaccessible to solvent; and it is conserved in all peripheral subunit-binding domains of other 2-oxo acid dehydrogenase complexes. This is in keeping with the general principle that buried hydrophilic residues are highly conserved. Since it presumably cannot play a direct role in recognition or be involved in binding either the E3 or the El subunits, we believe it to be conserved on structural as opposed to functional grounds. The absence of any nearby side-chains carrying positively charged groups means that no internal salt bridge can be formed. Using the iterative procedure described above, two side-chain-main-chain hydrogen bond restraints were found and applied to the carboxylate moiety; namely, Thr24 NH-Asp34 $0^{\delta\bar{2}}$ and Leu3l NH-Asp34 $\dot{\wp}^{\mathfrak{d}2}$ . The structures calculated with these restraints contained two more sidechain-main-chain hydrogen bonds involving the carboxylate side-chain: Gly23 NH-Asp34 $0^{\delta\mathbf{\breve{i}}}$ and Gly25 NH-Asp34 $\mathbf{0}^{\delta2}$ (Fig. ll). The new hydrogen bonds were in accord with the experimental data, which had indicated that the amide protons of Gly23 and Gly25 were subject to reduced rates of exchange. In addition a new side-chain-side-chain hydrogen bond from $\mathrm{Thr24~H^{\gamma1}}$ to Asp34 $0^{\vartheta2}$ was found in nearly one third of the structures. This correlated with the $3J_{\alpha\beta}$ coupling constant data which, given the local structure, implied that the hydroxyl group was buried. The solvent-inaccessible hydroxyl group requires stabilization and this is provided by hydrogen bond formation: the proton, $\bar{\mathbf{H}}^{\mathfrak{p1}}$ , with Asp34 $\boldsymbol{0}^{\tilde{\delta}2}$ and the oxygen atom, $\bar{\mathbf{\uprho}}^{\mathbf{\upmu}}$ , by way of an intra-residue hydrogen bond with the main-chain amide proton. Asp34 and Thr24 are crucial in defining the structure of the loop from its beginning at Gln22, immediately after the $\mathbf{3_{10}}$ -helix, to its end as defined by Leu3l, just before helix II.
The asymmetric character of the: hydrogen-bond network formed by the carboxylate side-chain of Asp34 may result in different C-O bond lengths. Such a subtle feature cannot be revealed by n.m.r. spectroscopic techniques and structure calculations. The quadruply hydrogen-bonded $\mathbf{0}^{\delta2}$ atom may bear the negative charge and, as it should have less electron density available for molecular orbital overlap, the $\mathrm{C}\mathrm{-}\bar{\mathrm{O}}^{\delta\bar{2}}$ bond may have more single-bond character and thus be longer than $\mathrm{C}{-}\mathsf{O}^{\delta\mathbf{1}}$ ，
## (v) Role of conserved glycine residues
The Gly23-Thr24-Gly25 and Gly28-Arg29 regions in the structured loop are found to be conserved throughout nearly all peripheral subunit-binding domains. The only mutations that occur are replacement of Thr24 by serine, which also provides a sidechain hydroxyl group available for hydrogen bonding, and the substitution of Arg29 by a lysine residue. The roles of the completely conserved residues Gly23, Gly25 and Gly28 are of considerable interest and we can comment on these since our calculated structures show convergence in this region, unlike those of the $\mathbf{E3}$ -binding domain from E. coli E2o (Robien et al., l992). The first point of interest is that none of the residues adopts positive values for the dihedral angle $\phi$ . The only other obvious reason for the conservation of a glycine residue is that the presence of $a$ side-chain would result in unfavourable steric clashes with the rest of the structure. However, the structure of the protein indicates that were side-chains present in these residues, they would point out into solution (Fig. l2). Thus they could potentially interfere with recognition of and binding to E3 or Elp if this region of structure were involved in the function of the protein. Moreover, the side-chains of Arg29. Leu3l and Glu33 all adopt similar solvent-exposed conformations, presenting a variety of potential sites for intermolecular interactions.
(vi) Comparison of the restrained minimized structure with the final population data set
A final restrained minimized structure, $\langle\S\mathbf{A}\rangle_{\mathbf{r}}.$ was determined by applying an energy minimization protocol_ to the mean simulated annealing structure, $\langle\S\mathbf{A}\rangle$ , which was a statistical average of the final population of 35 simulated annealing structures. The minimization procedure involved the introduction of electrostatic, hydrogen bond and Lennard-Jones interactions and was used to relieve strain, remove bad contacts and restore good covalent geometry after averaging. Before averaging atoms in the calculated structures were named according to IUPAC nomenclature. This states that in the case of atoms covalently bonded to a trigonal carbon atom, priority is given to the atom with the smaller value of the appropriate dihedral angle, x, which can be determined only once the conformation of the side-chain is known. This is important for ordered (buried and/or hydrogen-bonded) residues specifically in the E3/Elp-binding domain,
Tyrl0, Aspl7, Asp34 and Phe38.The structure validation procedure was performed by using the program NAOMI.
Not all of the hydrogen bonds present in the simulated annealing structures are found in the restrained minimized structure. The non-polar contacts are observed to be reasonably representative of those present in the simulated annealing structures. To gain a full picture of the structural features, the restrained minimized structure should be analysed in conjunction with the simulated annealing structures.
The statistical comparison of the restrained minimized structure with the 35 final simulated structures and the average structure is given in Table 2. A Lennard-Jones van der Waals energy of - 105-4 kcal $\mathbf{mol}^{-1}$ ！ $\mathbf{\dot{\mathbf{!}}\ c a l=4{\cdot}l84\cdot}\mathbf{J})$ was calculated by using the CHARMm force field (Brooks et $a l$ ‘ 1983). The mean $(\phi,\psi)$ values for helix I and helix II are $\{-62{\cdot}2\{\pm}13{\cdot}7\}^{\circ}$ ， $-37\cdot1\exists(\pm11\cdot1)^{\circ};$ and $(-63{\cdot}0(\pm12{\cdot}2)^{\circ}$ 冏→ $-41{\cdot}3(\pm16{\cdot}8)^{\circ};$ respectively; these are in close agreement with values found in other high resolution structures (Barlow & Thornton, 1988). The $\mathbf{3_{10}}$ -helix is distorted towards an $\alpha$ -helical conformation as evidenced by mean $(\phi,\psi)$ values of $(-49\cdot9(\pm6\cdot3)^{\circ}$ ²， $-433(\pm8\cdot7)^{\circ})$ , respectively. All residues apart from Glyl5, which is in an $x_{L}$ conformation, lie in regions of conformational space with negative $\phi$ . It is worth noting that both Lys26 and Asn27 which, in some of the simulated annealing structures were in regions of positive $\phi$ possess negative $\phi$ in the restrained minimized structure. Helix I contains a network of bifurcated $i,i+3$ and $i,i+4$ hydrogen bonds, whereas helix II contains exclusively $i,i+4$ hydrogen bonds except for the $i,i+3$ bond formed between Ile35 CQ and Phe38 NH. As in the simulated annealing structures, no hydrogen bond is observed between Phe38 NH and Asp34 CO. Both pairs of overlapping $\beta$ -turns are present and adopt the same conformation as the most heavily populated subset of the simulated annealing structures.
## 4. Conclusions
All regions of the peripheral subunit-binding domain of $B$ . stearothermophilus are highly ordered. This is of unusual interest as the structured region (residues Val7 to Leu39) comprises only 33 residues. Privalov & Gill (l988) have proposed that the lower limit for a peptide with a stable folded structure is 50 residues. The E3/Elp-binding domain has thus overcome the inherent disadvantage of having a high surface-to-volume ratio encountered by small peptides. To our knowledge this is the smallest independently folded protein domain, possessing no disulphide bridges, ligands or cofactors, whose structure has been determined. Given the surpris ingly high degree of order of the structure, it was thought possible that intermolecular protein-protein interactions were contributing towards the stability. Such phenomena, however, seem highly improbable, since NOESY spectra recorded at different concentrations, ranging from I'5 to $\mathrm{5~mm}$ of the synthetic peptide failed to show any deviation in either the spectral line-width or the observed chemical shifts of the protons in the NH $c^{\alpha}$ fingerprint region (data not shown). We believe that the tw) buried, highly conserved, hydrophilic residues Asp34 and Thr24, are crucial to the stability. The topological arrangement of the three small helices (2 $\alpha$ -helices, and a $\mathbf{\bar{3}_{10}}$ -helix) mirrors that of larger three-helix proteins where the packing arrangement is well described by an octahedron (Murzin & Finkelstein, 1988).
An important question, which we cannot yet answer unequivocally, is why it was possible to obtainhydrogen exchangedata for the $B$ . stearothermophilus $\mathrm{\bfE2p}$ domain described here but. not for the E. coli E2o domain (Robien et al., 1992). Since the stability of the folded state versus the unfolded state is known to be small (5 to $\mathbf{\left|0\right.}_{\mathrm{{\tiny:\equiv}}}{\mathrm{{\bf{\vec{\alpha}}\mathrm{{al}}}}}\mathbf{m}\mathbf{\alpha}\mathbf{\alpha}^{-1}$ ) and, furthermore, is the difference between two large numbers, very slight changes in sequence may have a large effect on the stability of the folded state (Alexander et al., 1992). For example, the two residues from the $\mathbf{3_{10}}$ -helix that make non-polar contacts in the B. stearothermophilus domain, Hlel8 and Leu20, are replaced in the $E$ . coli domain by alanine residues, and in helix II, Phe38 is replaced by histidine.
In the case of short peptide sequences, hydrophobic residues that lie outside secondary structural elements can contribute to the hydrophobic core, as in larger proteins. However, in smaller proteins this role may be far more significant in stabilizing the folc. In the B. stearothermophilus E3/Elp-binding domain, where the parallel helices pack against each other asymmetrically, residues outside the helices, but in close proximity to them, for example Vall6 and Val30, are involved in the formation of a stable non-polar interior. This may have implications for the de novo design of small proteins, where it may not be possible to construct small, well-defined structures by simple packing of secondary structural elements against each other (DeGrado et al., 1991).
Further, the solvent-accessibility data for the E3-binding domain show that the hydroxyl group of Thr24 is exposed to solvent, whereas in the E3/Elp-binding domain it is buried. The observation that Thr24 is rarely substituted and then only conservatively by serine, implies that the buried hydroxyl group should be a factor in determining the local structure in the loop region. The structure of the E3/Elp-binding domain confirms the hypothesis that Asp34, which is conserved in all E3- and E3/Elp-binding domains and which is located in the non-polar interior of the structure, stabilizes the loop region through hydrogen-bond formation between its side-chain carboxylate group and the main-chain amide protons of Gly23, Thr24, Gly25 and Leu3l, and the side-chain hydroxyl group of Thr24. For the E2o E3-binding domain it was proposed, in the absence of exchange data, that hydrogen bonds may be possible between Asp34 and both Thr24 and Thr3l (Robien et al., 1992).
In summary, the structures of the E3/Elp- and E3-binding domains are very similar, but it is possible that there are differences between them. We cannot yet explain the differences in the way El is bound in icosahedral and octahedral 2-oxo acid dehydrogenase complexes. It will be necessary to look carefully at the structure of the peripheral subunit-binding domain in conjunction with the structure of its associated catalytic domain to see how these differences can be resolved.
The co-ordinates of the restrained minimized structure and those of the 35 simulated annealing structures have been deposited in the Brookhaven Data Bank, under accession numbers lPDE and 1PDD, respectively.
